From 4a68166ca9d986c03575c94563aafe72b987d1a8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 19:10:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Integration by batches: review of lot L2

An independent review found that the thermodynamic forces in the global
frame kept by the material were stale when the integration and the
computation of the inner forces alternate element by element (assembly
with PETSc): the writes of MGIS through its views do not change the
versions of the arrays. The cache and the versions are removed.

- Inner forces: the thermodynamic forces are rotated point by point, as
  before lot L2, when the behaviour provides the rotation of one point.
  For an orthotropic behaviour generated by the generic-parallel interface,
  which only provides the rotations of batches, they are computed at all
  the points by `integrateBatch`, after the integration, or on demand
  after an integration point by point or a revert
  (`BehaviourIntegratorBase::getRotatedThermodynamicForces`).
- `Material::computeGradientsInGlobalFrame` and
  `computeThermodynamicForcesInGlobalFrame`: computed at each call, in
  both layouts, by the post-processings (mean values, rotated evaluators,
  which keep their own values).
- `integrateBatch`: the material properties at the beginning of the time
  step are replaced by views on the ones at the end of the time step and
  restored by a guard (no copy, no reallocation of the arrays held by the
  material); the tangent operator blocks are only rotated on success;
  refused for a behaviour integrator without support of the integration by
  batches; documented difference of the initial time step scaling factor.
- Documentation of `integrateBatch` (MGIS stops at the first failure for a
  behaviour generated by the generic interface); message naming the
  environment variable for an invalid default value.

Tests: inner forces computed element by element just after the
integration of each element compared to the ones computed after the
integration of all the elements (the scenario of the review); the
behaviours generated by the generic-parallel interface are now compared to
the integration point by point with the rotations of one point, which
checks the rotations of all the points independently; plane strain (2D
rotation matrix), prediction and FBar for orthotropic behaviours; rotations
in the global frame of a finite strain behaviour in both layouts.
139/139, 133/139 in the Batch mode (refusals of the micromorphic behaviour
integrators).
